     The kernel ffiirrmmwwaarree__rreeggiisstteerr firmware API is made of the following func‐
     tions:

     ffiirrmmwwaarree__rreeggiisstteerr() registers with the kernel an image of size ddaattaassiizzee
     located at address ddaattaa, under the name iimmaaggeennaammee.

     The function returns NULL on error (e.g. because an image with the same
     name already exists, or the image table is full), or a _c_o_n_s_t _s_t_r_u_c_t
     _f_i_r_m_w_a_r_e _* pointer to the image requested.

     ffiirrmmwwaarree__uunnrreeggiisstteerr() tries to unregister the firmware image iimmaaggeennaammee
     from the system.  The function is successful and returns 0 if there are
     no pending references to the image, otherwise it does not unregister the
     image and returns EBUSY.

     ffiirrmmwwaarree__ggeett() and ffiirrmmwwaarree__ggeett__ffllaaggss() return the requested firmware im‐
     age.  The _f_l_a_g_s argument may be set to FIRMWARE_GET_NOWARN to indicate
     that errors on firmware load or registration should only be logged in
     case of bboooovveerrbboossee.  If the image is not yet registered with the system,
     the functions try to load it.  This involves the linker subsystem and
     disk access, so ffiirrmmwwaarree__ggeett() or ffiirrmmwwaarree__ggeett__ffllaaggss() must not be called
     with any locks (except for _G_i_a_n_t).  Note also that if the firmware image
     is loaded from a filesystem it must already be mounted.  In particular
     this means that it may be necessary to defer requests from a driver at‐
     tach method unless it is known the root filesystem is already mounted.

     On success, ffiirrmmwwaarree__ggeett() and ffiirrmmwwaarree__ggeett__ffllaaggss() return a pointer to
     the image description and increase the reference count for this image.
     On failure, the functions return NULL.

     ffiirrmmwwaarree__ppuutt() drops a reference to a firmware image.  The _f_l_a_g_s argument
     may be set to FIRMWARE_UNLOAD to indicate that firmware_put is free to
     reclaim resources associated with the firmware image if this is the last
     reference.  By default a firmware image will be deferred to a
     taskqueue(9) thread so the call may be done while holding a lock.  In
     certain cases, such as on driver detach, this cannot be allowed.

