     As mentioned before, any component of the system can register firmware
     images at any time by simply calling ffiirrmmwwaarree__rreeggiisstteerr().

     This is typically done when a module containing a firmware image is given
     control, whether compiled in, or preloaded by //bboooott//llooaaddeerr, or manually
     loaded with kldload(8).  However, a system can implement additional mech‐
     anisms to bring these images in memory before calling
     ffiirrmmwwaarree__rreeggiisstteerr().

     When ffiirrmmwwaarree__ggeett() or ffiirrmmwwaarree__ggeett__ffllaaggss() does not find the requested
     image, it tries to load it using one of the available loading mechanisms.
     At the moment, there is only one, namely LLooaaddaabbllee kkeerrnneell mmoodduulleess.

     A firmware image named ffoooo is looked up by trying to load the module
     named ffoooo..kkoo, using the facilities described in kld(4).  In particular,
     images are looked up in the directories specified by the sysctl variable
     kkeerrnn..mmoodduullee__ppaatthh which on most systems defaults to
     //bboooott//kkeerrnneell;;//bboooott//mmoodduulleess.

     Note that in case a module contains multiple images, the caller should
     first request a ffiirrmmwwaarree__ggeett() or ffiirrmmwwaarree__ggeett__ffllaaggss() for the first im‐
     age contained in the module, followed by requests for the other images.

BBUUIILLDDIINNGG FFIIRRMMWWAARREE LLOOAADDAABBLLEE MMOODDUULLEESS
     A firmware module is built by embedding the ffiirrmmwwaarree iimmaaggee into a suit‐
     able loadable kernel module that calls ffiirrmmwwaarree__rreeggiisstteerr() on loading,
     and ffiirrmmwwaarree__uunnrreeggiisstteerr() on unloading.

     Various system scripts and makefiles let you build a module by simply
     writing a Makefile with the following entries:


             KMOD=   imagename
             FIRMWS= image_file:imagename[:version]
             .include <bsd.kmod.mk>

     where KMOD is the basename of the module; FIRMWS is a list of colon-sepa‐
     rated tuples indicating the image_file's to be embedded in the module,
     the imagename and version of each firmware image.

     If you need to embed firmware images into a system, you should write ap‐
     propriate entries in the <files.arch> file, e.g. this example is from
     ssyyss//aarrmm//xxssccaallee//iixxpp442255//ffiilleess..iixxpp442255:

     ixp425_npe_fw.c                         optional npe_fw                 \
             compile-with    "${AWK} -f $S/tools/fw_stub.awk                 \
                             IxNpeMicrocode.dat:npe_fw -mnpe -c${.TARGET}"   \
             no-implicit-rule before-depend local                            \
             clean           "ixp425_npe_fw.c"
     #
     # NB: ld encodes the path in the binary symbols generated for the
     #     firmware image so link the file to the object directory to
     #     get known values for reference in the _fw.c file.
     #
     IxNpeMicrocode.fwo  optional npe_fw                                     \
             dependency      "IxNpeMicrocode.dat"                            \
             compile-with    "${LD} -b binary -d -warn-common                \
                                 -r -d -o ${.TARGET} IxNpeMicrocode.dat"     \
             no-implicit-rule                                                \
             clean           "IxNpeMicrocode.fwo"

     Firmware was previously committed to the source tree as uuencoded files,
     but this is no longer required; the binary firmware file should be com‐
     mitted to the tree as provided by the vendor.

     Note that generating the firmware modules in this way requires the avail‐
     ability of the following tools: awk(1), make(1), the compiler and the
     linker.

